{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-7","id":"msg_bdrk_5wbiqzmxyqajxnfflapw7f4wohsbyby6mtyhnnv7mf6ybzb55f5q","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"All 5 tests pass. The implementation is complete:\n\n## Summary\n\nI implemented the C-to-Rust translation for the Frame-of-Reference (FOR) integer compression library.\n\n**Key design decisions:**\n\n1. **`src/forLib.rs`** — Translated the high-level API directly from `c_src/for.c`:\n   - `for_compress_*`, `for_uncompress`, `for_select`, `for_linear_search`, `for_lower_bound_search`, `for_append_*`, plus the `_bits` variants, `for_compressed_size_*`, and the `required_bits` helper\n   - Uses `to_le_bytes` / `from_le_bytes` instead of unsafe pointer casts to handle the metadata header (4-byte little-endian min, 1-byte bit count)\n   - The `for_append_impl` function uses `Vec<u32>` instead of the C `malloc`/`free` heap allocation\n\n2. **`src/for_gen.rs`** — Replaced ~28k lines of hand-unrolled C bit-packing routines with a small generic pack/unpack/linear-search core. Each public `packN_M`/`unpackN_M`/`linsearchN_M` function delegates to the generic implementation with the appropriate `count` and `bits` parameters. The strange-looking source signatures (e.g. `unpack5_8(base, &[u32], &mut [u8])`) are preserved as required, and a private `unpack_dispatch`/`linsearch_dispatch` module provides natural-signature helpers used by `forLib.rs`.\n\n3. **No `unsafe`, no FFI** — pure safe Rust throughout. All bit manipulation goes through standard `u32`/`u8` arithmetic with explicit `wrapping_*` operations to match C overflow semantics.\n\n**Verification:** Added `tests/correctness.rs` mirroring the C `test.c` test suite (`highlevel_sorted`, `highlevel_unsorted`, `append_sorted`, `append_sorted_bignum`, `append_unsorted`). All 5 tests pass in release mode."}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":513,"cache_r